    "case_id": "539",
    "case_summary": "A 71-year-old man presented with painful lower-extremity nodules and polyarthralgia developing over several months. Medical history included recurrent bladder cancer post-resection and local chemotherapy (complicated by bladder-wall perforation and intraperitoneal mitomycin leak requiring surgical management), coronary artery disease, essential hypertension with diastolic dysfunction, and tobacco use. No previous history of gastrointestinal disease or significant family history of rheumatologic conditions or cancer.\n\nPatient had painful nodules on the anterior shins and inner left thigh. The left wrist, hand, and metacarpophalangeal joints were edematous and tender to palpation, with marked synovitis. Bilateral lower extremities were edematous with varicosities, and toes and dorsal feet were edematous and exquisitely tender. No jaundice, scleral icterus, or abdominal tenderness was noted, and patient was tolerating a full diet.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Serum lipase >20,000 U/L    * Amylase 7,914 U/L    * Extensive autoimmune and infectious work-up negative (including ANA, RF, CCP, HLA-B27, HIV, Hepatitis panels)    * Cancer markers: CA 19-9: 118 U/L, CEA: 2.6 ng/mL  - Imaging Studies:    * Initial CT with contrast and MRCP: Multiple cystic lesions within pancreatic head    * EUS findings: 1.9 × 1.7-cm mass in portal vein with extension to superior mesenteric vein    * CT pancreas phase and MRI: Confirmed connection between pancreatic duct and adjacent vasculature      - Pathology:    * Cutaneous lesion biopsy: 'Ghost cells'—fat necrosis with saponification    * Synovial fluid aspirate: No crystals, leukocytes, or bacteria on gram stain",
